<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Description>
      FlowX RabbitMQ adapters: an event publisher that drains the transactional outbox to a
      topic exchange with the event type as the routing key and publisher confirms on, and a
      bus consumer serving one quorum queue per subscription with manual acknowledgement and a
      dead-letter exchange.
    </Description>
    <PackageTags>flowx;rabbitmq;amqp;outbox;bus;durable-execution;dead-letter</PackageTags>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Pinned exactly, like every other dependency in this repository. The client is the only
      thing this package needs beyond the contracts: ADR-0009 says a plugin depends on
      FlowX.Abstractions and on whatever it talks to, and nothing else.

      7.2.1 rather than 6.x because 6.x has no asynchronous publish at all — BasicPublish is
      void and a confirm is awaited through a separate WaitForConfirms call — and ADR-0072
      turns the confirm into the thing PublishAsync's return value means. The surface used
      here is the static half of the library: IChannel, BasicProperties, byte arrays and a
      Dictionary<string, object?> of headers. Nothing is reflective and nothing is serialised
      by the client, which is what keeps constraint C2 satisfied without a suppression.

      Its licence is 'Apache-2.0 OR MPL-2.0', a disjunction: this repository takes the
      Apache-2.0 half, which is the whole of why it is permissible here. docs/DEPENDENCIES.md
      §1.1 carries that argument and the gate reads it.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="RabbitMQ.Client" Version="7.2.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="10.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      A plugin depends on the runtime; the runtime never depends on a plugin
      (RuntimeDoesNotReferenceAnyPlugin). This one depends on less than that: IEventPublisher,
      IBusConsumer, OutboxRecord, BusMessage, Result and Error all live in FlowX.Abstractions,
      so a broker adapter needs nothing from Core, Runtime or Hosting — and nothing at all
      from plugins/FlowX.Redis, which is the point of the package: two transports that share
      a conformance suite and no source.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/FlowX.Abstractions/FlowX.Abstractions.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
